{
  "id": "mill_bandits",
  "name": "Trouble at the Old Mill",
  "giver": "Elowen",
  "faction": "town",
  "starts_on_new_game": true,
  "chapter": 1,
  "chapter_title": "The Old Mill",
  "desc": "Millbrook has no flour, and Elowen wants to know why.",
  "xp": 40,
  "rewards": {
    "gold": 150,
    "items": [
      [
        "steel_longsword",
        1
      ]
    ],
    "rep": [
      "town",
      15
    ],
    "returns": [
      {
        "item": "toll_purse",
        "gold": 40,
        "rep": 5
      }
    ]
  },
  "stages": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "journal": "Elowen the Trader called me over in Millbrook square. Something about flour.",
      "objective": "Talk to Elowen",
      "finishes": false,
      "condition": {
        "kind": "talk",
        "target": "Elowen",
        "count": 1
      },
      "say": "Eight sacks a week, I sell. Today I've {shelf_flour} on the shelf, and it isn't for want of asking. Ever since bandits took the Old Mill, the Cross sends its runner to Mara's windmill, and Millbrook gets whatever's left. Ask Mara. She'll tell you.",
      "call": "You! New face! Have you got a minute?"
    },
    {
      "index": 10,
      "journal": "Elowen has no flour to sell. Since bandits took the Old Mill, Hollin's Cross buys from Mara's windmill and Millbrook goes short. She told me to ask Mara.",
      "objective": "Ask Mara about the flour",
      "finishes": false,
      "condition": {
        "kind": "talk",
        "target": "Mara",
        "count": 1
      },
      "say": "Sails don't turn on wind alone. They turn on wheat, and nobody's bringing me enough. There's {mill_wheat} wheat in the hopper and {mill_flour} flour in the loft, and the Cross's man is first in line for it. The Old Mill, north-east of here, used to grind for the Cross. Bandits sit in it now. Get them out, and the carts go back where they belong."
    },
    {
      "index": 20,
      "journal": "Mara's windmill can't grind for two villages. The Old Mill north-east of Millbrook used to take the Cross's grain, until bandits moved in.",
      "objective": "Find the Old Mill",
      "finishes": false,
      "condition": {
        "kind": "visit",
        "target": "Old Mill",
        "count": 1
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 30,
      "journal": "I found the Old Mill. The bandits there answer to a man called Hal Corbin; the mill will not turn while he holds it.",
      "objective": "Kill Hal Corbin at the Old Mill",
      "finishes": false,
      "condition": {
        "kind": "kill",
        "target": "Hal Corbin",
        "count": 1
      },
      "waypoint": "Hal Corbin"
    },
    {
      "index": 40,
      "journal": "Hal Corbin is dead. A man who robs a mill for a living keeps accounts; his ledger should be on him.",
      "objective": "Take Hal Corbin's ledger",
      "finishes": false,
      "condition": {
        "kind": "fetch",
        "target": "corbin_ledger",
        "count": 1
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 50,
      "journal": "Corbin's ledger: flour sold \"to the boat\", silver paid at the south shore, \"the Ash-men's price, settled\". Aldric the Reeve keeps the mill tolls at Hollin's Cross. He should read this.",
      "objective": "Take the ledger to Aldric at Hollin's Cross",
      "finishes": false,
      "condition": {
        "kind": "talk",
        "target": "Aldric",
        "count": 1
      },
      "say": "Corbin's book? Give it here. \"Flour out, eleven sacks, to the boat.\" \"Silver, for mill-work, paid at the south shore.\" The south shore means Mirrormere, and a boat on Mirrormere means someone on the water saw it. Old Maud at Reedhollow sees every boat that crosses. The Cross can buy its own grain again, and Millbrook's shelves should fill.",
      "heard": {
        "death": "Hal Corbin",
        "say": "Word got here before you did: Hal Corbin's dead at the Old Mill, and folk are saying who did it. Is that his book? Give it here. \"Flour out, eleven sacks, to the boat.\" \"Silver, for mill-work, paid at the south shore.\" The south shore means Mirrormere, and a boat on Mirrormere means someone on the water saw it. Old Maud at Reedhollow sees every boat that crosses."
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 60,
      "journal": "Aldric paid me for Hal Corbin and his ledger. With the Old Mill's bandits gone, the roads are clear and Millbrook's shelves should fill. The flour went south, to Mirrormere; Old Maud at Reedhollow sees every boat on that water.",
      "objective": "Aldric paid me for Hal Corbin and his ledger.",
      "finishes": true
    }
  ]
}
